In test 2 ("live probe agrees → defer stands") I returned raw cachedPrs() from listOpenPrsLive — but the live re-read in production returns the same shape that github_list_prs returns — and queryPrs maps via prLabels. So the probe's live items should ALSO be raw REST shape... wait, in rederiveHoldersLive, liveItems are passed to mutexRegistryModule.derive directly AND to mutexHoldersOf(liveItems, q). mutexHoldersOf expects items with plain-string labels. In production, listOpenPrsLive returns the raw github_list_prs payload — labels as objects? What does the real github_list_prs return — labels as [{name}] objects presumably (REST). Hmm, but the ORIGINAL code called provider.githubApi('list_prs', ...) and fed the result through asItemsArray → same raw shape. So mutexHoldersOf on live items would see object labels and never match — a real bug in the original code too!

Wait — actually in the probe path, labels shape: GitHub REST /pulls returns labels as array of {name:...} objects. But `labelHoldersOf` in mutexRegistry checks `labels.indexOf('ai_validating')` — plain strings. And mutexHoldersOf similarly. So live items with object labels yield ZERO label holders → liveHolderSet.holders=[] → phantom detected always → self-heal fires every time → holders cleared → mutex never defers! That would be a live bug: the phantom-heal would consider every defer a phantom and clear holders, breaking the mutex serialization entirely.

Hmm wait — what does github_list_prs actually return through the dmtools bridge? Maybe it normalizes labels to strings. The githubSource item mapping uses prLabels(p) with `(l && l.name) || l` — tolerant of both. The test fixtures in test 399 use [{name:...}] objects. And test suite 'gh-100 phantom-heal helpers' uses pr(n, labels) with plain string arrays like ['ai_validating'].

To be robust, the live probe should normalize live item labels to strings — just like queryPrs does with prLabels. Fix: in rederiveHoldersLive (or livePrItems), map live items to normalize labels:

```js
function liveItemsNormalized(liveItems) {
   return (liveItems || []).map(function (p) {
       return { number: p.number, labels: prLabels(p) };
   });
}
```
But careful: derive() reads it.labels with indexOf — plain strings OK; also it.number. mutexHoldersOf reads .labels and .number. So normalizing to {number, labels:[names]} is sufficient.

