The cap test: signature alarms kept the newest 3 — after 5 pushes with dedupe-per-record... Let me trace: recordPhantomHeal filters out same-signature alarms then pushes the new one, then caps per signature to 3 (keeping FIRST 3 in list order = newest first, since filter removed olds and push appends at end — wait: alarms after filter = other-signature alarms only; push adds the new one at END. capAlarmsPerSignature iterates in order and keeps first ≤3 per signature — the newest is LAST. So for same-signature sequence: each iteration state has ≤1 same-sig (others filtered), push → the same-sig entries in list: [older from other ticks? no — filtered]. Hmm, in my loop I merge via mergeSnapshot each tick: snap.alarms accumulates across ticks (prev.alarms carried). Tick i: recordPhantomHeal(snap,...) — snap.alarms has the previous (i-1) alarms (all sig 997 — wait, after tick 1, snap.alarms=[a1]. Tick 2: recordPhantomHeal filters same-sig (a1) → push a2 → cap → [a2]. So state only ever keeps ONE per signature! Then mergeSnapshot(snap, {alarm: a2}) → prev.alarms [a2] concat new a2, dedupe by key... key = type|cached|at — a2 pushed twice? prev.alarms=[a2], e.alarm=a2 (st.alarms[last]=a2) → dedupe keeps one → alarms=[a2].

So the per-signature cap keeps only the newest ONE — never accumulates. That matches "drop the matching-signature entry" semantics of recordPhantomHeal. So the list can never hold more than 1 per signature via this path. When would multiple same-signature alarms coexist? appendDedupedAlarms merges prev.alarms (caried) + new alarm — but prev alarms went through recordPhantomHeal's filter... which drops same-sig. Hmm — recordPhantomHeal drops matching-signature THEN pushes: so per-signature count ≤ 1 always at record time. The cap therefore only bites when prev.alarms ALREADY contains ≥3 same-signature records that weren't filtered — e.g., alarms built by mergeSnapshot from tick EVENTS (githubSource path): each tick event's alarm carries signature cached holders; if the cached holder set is the same across ticks, the alarms accumulate in the published tail via appendDedupedAlarms — those have distinct `at` keys so no dedupe. THAT's where the cap matters: a flapping registry with the same phantom signature every tick would grow the tail up to MAX_ALARMS (5) — per-signature cap limits to 3.

So my test scenario is wrong. Correct test: build prev alarms by merging tick events directly:

```js
